WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.500
One sort of vowel used in colloquial Tamil that is not found in English, though familiar to speakers of say French, is nasal vowels.

00:07.500 --> 00:14.000
Practice these sounds by repeating the words that follow. The English meaning is given first.

00:14.000 --> 00:16.000
May come.

00:16.000 --> 00:18.000
வரலாம் (varalaam)

00:19.000 --> 00:20.500
He came.

00:20.500 --> 00:22.500
வந்தான் (vandaan)

00:23.500 --> 00:24.500
Tree.

00:24.500 --> 00:26.500
மரம் (maram)

00:27.500 --> 00:29.000
We came.

00:29.000 --> 00:31.000
வந்தோம் (vandoom)

00:32.000 --> 00:33.500
He.

00:33.500 --> 00:35.500
அவன் (avan)

00:36.500 --> 00:37.500
I came.

00:37.500 --> 00:40.000
வந்தேன் (vandeen)

00:41.500 --> 00:43.000
It will come.

00:43.000 --> 00:45.000
வரும் (varum)

00:47.000 --> 00:59.500
If these words are made into questions by the addition of the sound 'aa', the vowels are no longer nasal. Instead, an 'm' or an 'n' as indicated in the written form is pronounced before the following vowel.

00:59.500 --> 01:03.500
Try the same words as questions by repeating the following.

01:04.000 --> 01:06.000
வரலாமா

01:08.000 --> 01:10.000
வந்தானா

01:12.000 --> 01:14.000
மரமா

01:16.000 --> 01:18.000
வந்தோமா

01:21.000 --> 01:23.000
அவனா

01:25.000 --> 01:27.000
வந்தேனா

01:29.000 --> 01:31.000
வருமா